<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>How the two payments work</h2>
<p>With replacement cost coverage, the carrier generally pays the actual cash value first: replacement cost minus depreciation, minus your deductible. The depreciation is held back until you repair or replace, then paid up to what you actually spend.</p>
<h2>California time limits</h2>
<p>Insurance Code §2051.5 generally gives you at least 12 months from the first actual cash value payment to collect the holdback, and at least 36 months after a loss tied to a declared state of emergency, with extensions for good cause. Your policy may give you longer. These are legal rules, so confirm them for your loss.</p>
<h2>Checking depreciation</h2>
<ul><li>Depreciation should reflect condition and life, not a flat rate for every item.</li><li>Some carriers depreciate labor; whether that is appropriate depends on the policy and the item.</li><li>Use the cap selector to match the maximum depreciation shown on your estimate.</li></ul>

<h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary>Why is my first check so much smaller than the estimate?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Because depreciation and the deductible are subtracted first. The holdback is paid after you replace or repair.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if my policy is actual cash value only?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Check the box. You won’t receive a holdback, so the first check is the total. Many FAIR Plan dwelling policies are written that way unless replacement cost was added.</p></div></details></div>
